---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import CommandOutput from '../../components/CommandOutput.astro';
---

<DocsLayout title="CLI builds" activeNav="/cli/builds">
  <h1>Builds</h1>
  <p>
    A build record is the platform's account of one build attempt: its profile, status, tags,
    log, and artifacts. What you may write to a build depends on how it reports — that
    distinction runs through everything below, so the three reporting modes have pages of
    their own:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/cli/builds/manual-builds">Manual builds</a> — your CI runs the build and reports status and logs</li>
    <li><a href="/cli/builds/automated-builds">Automated builds</a> — a pipeline provider drives the build and reports for you</li>
    <li><a href="/cli/builds/eas-builds">EAS builds</a> — Expo runs the build and the record mirrors it</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds list         [--app APP] [--page N]
appmctl builds get          [--app APP] BUILD_ID
appmctl builds create       [--app APP] --profile TYPE [options]
appmctl builds cancel       [--app APP] BUILD_ID
appmctl builds patch        [--app APP] BUILD_ID [--status STATUS] [--tag NAME[:#HEX]]
appmctl builds wait         [--app APP] BUILD_ID [--timeout SECONDS]
appmctl builds when-running [--app APP] BUILD_ID -- COMMAND [ARG ...]`} />

  <h3>Lifecycle aliases</h3>
  <p>
    Manual-build status updates have short singular aliases. Omit <code>BUILD_ID</code>
    when the runner already exports <code>APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID</code>:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl build start     [BUILD_ID] [--app APP] # status: running
appmctl build completed [BUILD_ID] [--app APP] # status: completed
appmctl build failed    [BUILD_ID] [--app APP] # status: failed`} />
  <p>
    These map directly to <code>builds patch BUILD_ID --status ...</code>, so the same
    manual-reporting restrictions apply. An explicit ID overrides
    <code>APP_MANAGER_BUILD_ID</code>; <code>--tag</code> may also be included when the
    status and tags should be updated together.
  </p>

  <h3>List</h3>
  <CommandOutput command="appmctl builds list" capture="builds-list" />
  <p>
    Newest first, 50 per page by default. <code>--page</code> walks back through history.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong><code>--status</code> does not filter.</strong> The CLI accepts it and sends it,
    but the API's build index takes no status filter, so the response comes back unfiltered.
    Filter on the client for now:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json builds list | jq -r '.data[] | select(.status == "failed") | .id'`} />

  <h3>Show one build</h3>
  <CommandOutput
    command="appmctl builds get 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5"
    capture="builds-get"
  />

  <h3>Create</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>--profile TYPE</code></td><td>Build profile: <code>production</code>, <code>appstore</code>, <code>development</code>, <code>enterprise</code>, <code>adhoc</code>, <code>preview</code>. Required.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--request-uuid UUID</code></td><td>Idempotency key. Generated for you when omitted; supply your own so a CI retry does not create a second build.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--context JSON</code></td><td>Arbitrary JSON recorded with the build, e.g. the commit and runner</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--reporting-mode MODE</code></td><td><code>manual</code> (default) or <code>eas</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--expo-build-id ID</code></td><td>Required when <code>--reporting-mode eas</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--app-identifier-id UUID</code></td><td>Target identifier, when the app has more than one for the profile</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>--tag NAME[:#HEX]</code></td><td>Attach a tag; repeatable</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds create --profile production \\
  --request-uuid "$(uuidgen)" \\
  --context '{"runner":"github-actions","commit":"a1b2c3d"}' \\
  --tag android:#3ddc84 --tag 1.4.0:#f59e0b`} />

  <h3>Tags</h3>
  <p>
    A tag is a name, optionally with a hex colour for its badge. On <code>patch</code> the
    flag replaces the build's whole tag set rather than adding to it, so send every tag you
    want to keep. Unlike status, tags may be set on any build regardless of reporting mode:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds patch 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5 --tag android:#3ddc84 --tag 1.4.0

# Replaces the set: after this the build has one tag
appmctl builds patch 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5 --tag nightly`} />
  <p>
    <code>patch</code> requires at least one of <code>--status</code> or <code>--tag</code>.
    Status transitions are limited to manually reported builds; see
    <a href="/cli/builds/manual-builds">Manual builds</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Cancel</h3>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds cancel 019fc789-47b9-7013-aadf-bf9444bdc86e`} />
  <p>
    Cancelling requests cancellation. A provider-driven build moves to
    <code>cancel_requested</code> until the provider acknowledges it.
  </p>

  <h2>Gating on a build</h2>
  <p>
    <code>wait</code> polls until the build reaches a terminal state and exits with a code
    that reflects the outcome, so a CI job can gate on it with no extra scripting:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds wait 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5 --timeout 3600`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Exit code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>Build completed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>Build failed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>Build was cancelled</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>124</code></td><td>Timeout exceeded; without <code>--timeout</code> it waits indefinitely</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Polling happens every <code>APP_MANAGER_POLLING_INTERVAL</code> seconds, default 10.</p>

  <h3><code>when-running</code></h3>
  <p>
    <code>when-running</code> checks the build once and runs the command only if the build is
    <code>pending</code>, <code>running</code>, or <code>cancel_requested</code>. While the
    command runs it keeps polling and kills the command if the build reaches a terminal state
    — useful for expensive steps that should stop the moment someone cancels the build:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl builds when-running "$BUILD_ID" -- ./scripts/upload-to-store.sh`} />
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Exit code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>the command's own</td><td>The command ran to completion and its status is passed through</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>130</code></td><td>The build was cancelled while the command ran; the command was terminated</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The build was already terminal, or its status could not be read</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>when-running</code> accepts <code>--timeout</code> but ignores it: it does not wait
    for a pending build to become active, so the flag changes nothing. Use
    <code>builds wait</code> when you need a bounded wait.
  </p>

  <h2>Logs</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl logs get  [--app APP] --build-id ID
appmctl logs post [--app APP] --build-id ID --content TEXT`} />
  <p>
    <code>logs get</code> prints the raw API response, so pull the text out with
    <code>jq</code> when you want to read it:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json logs get --build-id "$BUILD_ID" | jq -r '.data.content'`} />
  <CommandOutput
    command="appmctl logs get --build-id 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5"
    capture="logs-get"
  />
  <p>
    <code>logs post</code> appends. For anything long-running prefer
    <code>appmctl stream</code>, which uploads while the command runs instead of after it
    finishes. The API caps a chunk at 256 KiB and a whole log at 5 MB.
  </p>

  <h2>Artifacts</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl artifacts list   [--app APP] --build-id ID
appmctl artifacts get    [--app APP] --build-id ID ARTIFACT_ID [-o FILE]
appmctl artifacts upload [--app APP] --build-id ID --name NAME [--metadata JSON] --file FILE
appmctl artifacts delete [--app APP] --build-id ID ARTIFACT_ID`} />
  <CommandOutput
    command="appmctl artifacts list --build-id 019fc789-47b5-7034-9291-4cd3c57b75d5"
    capture="artifacts-list"
  />
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl artifacts upload --build-id "$BUILD_ID" \\
  --name app-release.aab \\
  --metadata '{"channel":"internal"}' \\
  --file build/app-release.aab

appmctl artifacts get --build-id "$BUILD_ID" 019fc789-47c3-7280-a953-5d12a8a9b884 -o app.aab`} />
  <p>
    Artifacts are scoped to a build and disappear with it. Anything that should outlive the
    build belongs in <a href="/cli/files">files</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Scopes</h2>
  <p>
    <code>builds:read</code> and <code>builds:write</code>, <code>logs:read</code> and
    <code>logs:write</code>, <code>artifacts:read</code> and <code>artifacts:write</code>.
    <code>wait</code> and <code>when-running</code> need only <code>builds:read</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
